{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's central syllogism is self-defeating: it defines 'frontier' via mutual exclusion (at least one critic must reject it), then treats the §11.3 flag—whose meaning is precisely the point at issue—as proof that the claim reached the frontier. That is circular: the conclusion (frontier status) is established by re-labeling the very signal the problem asked the target to justify. The target thus presupposes what it purports to demonstrate, so the flag is sustained on a pretext, not by adjudication.","premise":"The §11.3 flag's evidentiary value is the very question the problem poses; asserting the flag is a 'boundary signature' of frontier-status uses that contested signal as its own justification.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"000000000000","quote":"a genuine frontier claim is, by definition, one that pushes beyond what at least one established critic can endorse. If both critics could pass it, it would not be at the frontier. The §11.3 flag is thus not a failure signal but a *boundary signature*: it marks the regime where the claim is genuinely novel."}],"target":"dfcc562d97853b4679aed7d501b01cff7e568cddb386fb1658df9f547a200d21"}]}